# libfido2's public headers and its .pc file, with nothing compiled.
#
# This is half of the answer to a cycle that looked unbreakable. libfido2
# hard-requires libudev on Linux -- CMakeLists.txt calls
# `pkg_search_module(UDEV libudev REQUIRED)` and src/hid_linux.c makes fourteen
# udev_* calls to enumerate hidraw at all -- and the only libudev in this
# distribution comes from systemd, a layer above. systemd in turn is built
# -Dlibfido2=enabled. Each appeared to need the other finished first.
#
# It is not symmetric. systemd never links libfido2: meson.build:1236 takes the
# dependency and line 1238 keeps only
# `partial_dependency(includes: true, compile_args: true)` from it, nothing
# else in the file touches the full object, and at runtime it dlopens
# "libfido2.so.1" and resolves 48 symbols by dlsym
# (src/shared/libfido2-util.c:93). So systemd needs these headers and this .pc
# at configure time and the shared object never -- while libfido2 needs libudev
# to compile at all. The arrow points one way, so the two halves are ordered
# rather than iterated: this member here, below systemd, and the real libfido2
# in losos-20-systemd after it.
#
# Nothing is compiled, so nothing is needed from udev, and that is the whole
# trick. src/CMakeLists.txt:151-152 installs fido.h and the fido/ directory as
# plain files straight out of the source tree; line 154 generates libfido2.pc
# from libfido2.pc.in with an @ONLY substitution of four cmake variables. The
# staging script reproduces exactly that and reads the version back out of the
# source so it is not written down a second time.
#
# It shares the LIBFIDO2 source key with the real recipe, the same way
# linux-headers shares LINUX with the kernel, so both come from one tarball and
# one hash and the headers can never describe a different version from the
# library. The `version:` list below is checked against manifest/sources.lock
# by tools/gates/versions.py independently of the other recipe's, so a
# `tools/check-latest --apply` that moved only one of the two turns the gate
# red instead of letting the pair drift apart quietly.
#
# Tombstone for the options this replaces: a two-pass systemd was the earlier
# recommendation and is not coming back unless this fails, because pass one
# leaves files behind that pass two may not overwrite, and it pays for a second
# full systemd compile -- pm has no build cache (C5, C6). eudev would put a
# systemd fork in a systemd-native distribution. Patching hid_linux.c would be
# carried forever. docs/yubikey.md and the recipe in losos-20-systemd carry the
# full list.
name: libfido2-headers
version:
- '1'
- '15'
- '0'
dependencies: []
steps:

- stage: Prepare
  name: fetch
  dl_urls:
    @URL_LIBFIDO2@: '@SHA256_LIBFIDO2@'
  run:
  - mkdir -p /build/src/libfido2-headers
  - tar -xf @DL_LIBFIDO2@ --no-same-owner -C /build/src/libfido2-headers --strip-components=1

- stage: Build
  name: install
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  # Both roots, because /dest is what this member contributes to the layer's
  # archive and /build/sysroot is what the members after it -- systemd, two
  # layers up -- actually configure against.
  - /bin/sh @RECIPE@/files/libfido2-headers/stage-headers.sh /build/src/libfido2-headers /dest /build/sysroot

- stage: Test
  name: staged
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  # fido.h is the umbrella header and fido/param.h is one of the ones behind
  # it, so a copy that silently took only the top level fails here.
  - test -e /dest/usr/include/fido.h
  - test -e /dest/usr/include/fido/param.h
  - test -e /dest/usr/lib/pkgconfig/libfido2.pc
  # The .pc's Version: line is not asserted here, and deliberately so. A pm
  # step has no shell -- pm splits the line itself -- so a grep for an anchored
  # pattern would arrive with its quotes attached and match nothing while
  # passing. The check lives in stage-headers.sh instead, which does have a
  # shell: it refuses to write the file at all if FIDO_MAJOR, FIDO_MINOR or
  # FIDO_PATCH came back empty from the source's CMakeLists.txt. Same
  # guarantee, in the one place that can express it.
